# Astro Conventions

This is a personal portfolio site built with Astro. It starts nearly empty on purpose. There are no components and no layouts yet. They get built during the workshop. Follow these conventions when generating or editing code.

## Project structure

```
src/
  components/    Reusable UI pieces. Empty at the start (has .gitkeep). Build them here.
  layouts/       Page shells that wrap content. Empty at the start. Create when needed.
  pages/         One file per route. index.astro is the homepage.
  content/       Content collections. Empty at the start. Set up when the work section is built.
  styles/        tokens.css (the design system) and global.css (a minimal baseline).
public/          Static assets served as-is (favicon, images).
```

## Core rules

- This is a content site, not an app. Default to static. Render everything at build time unless there is a clear reason not to.
- Prefer `.astro` components. Only reach for a framework island when a piece genuinely needs client-side interactivity, like a theme toggle. Keep any island as small as possible. Do not make a whole page an island.
- Keep components small and single-purpose. A `Nav` renders the navigation. A `Footer` renders the footer. A `ProjectCard` renders one project. Compose pages from these.
- Layouts are the shared HTML document shell (`<html>`, `<head>`, metadata, global CSS, `<body>`, and a `<slot />` for page content). Components are reusable UI pieces. Once a layout exists, pages should be thin: pick a layout, pass content into the slot.
- You can have more than one layout later (for example a base site layout and a wider case-study layout). Prefer that over copying `<head>` into every page.

## Build a page first, then extract components

Early in the workshop the About page is built as a single `index.astro` file, with no components. That is intentional. Do not pre-emptively componentise a page the user is still building by hand.

When asked to "turn this into components", "make the nav reusable", or similar (Loop 4), do this extraction:

1. Move the navigation markup into `src/components/Nav.astro`.
2. Move the footer markup into `src/components/Footer.astro`.
3. Create `src/layouts/BaseLayout.astro` as the document shell: `<html>`, `<head>` (charset, viewport, title), the global CSS import, `<body>`, then `<Nav />`, a `<slot />` for page content, and `<Footer />`. Accept a `title` prop (or similar) so each page can set its document title.
4. Update existing pages to use that layout. Keep page-specific content on the page.
5. If asked for a small shared control (Loop 4 mini activity), add `src/components/Button.astro` — a token-styled link or button with props such as `href`, label (slot or prop), and an optional variant. Use it in a couple of places. Do not invent a large component library.

Do not invent extra wrappers or folders unless asked. The point is one change to the nav (or button) updating everywhere it appears.

## Styling

- Use the design tokens in `src/styles/tokens.css`. Reference the semantic layer only. See the design-tokens rule.
- Never hard-code colours, sizes, or font families. This applies to single pages as much as components.
- Scoped `<style>` blocks inside `.astro` files are fine and preferred for component-specific styling. They should still reference tokens.

## When unsure

- Ask whether something needs to be interactive before adding an island. For a portfolio the answer is usually no.
- Match the patterns already in the repo. As components get built, follow their style.
- Keep the build fast and the output simple. A portfolio should load quickly and be easy to maintain.
